<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <!--
    App Attest (§2.3). The device holds a Secure Enclave key, not an API key;
    an assertion from that key is what the proxy exchanges for a 15-minute
    token.

    `development` accepts attestations from debug and TestFlight builds. The
    proxy has a matching `APP_ATTEST_ENV` var, and **both must be flipped to
    `production` before shipping** — the proxy's `wrangler.jsonc` says the
    same thing from the other side. A production proxy that still accepts
    development attestations would let a debug build mint tokens against the
    real API key.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.developer.devicecheck.appattest-environment</key>
  <string>development</string>

  <!--
    Shared with the Share Extension. §4.1 makes the extension the only general
    route for content from other apps, and it runs in its own sandbox — so the
    SwiftData store has to live in a container both processes can reach. See
    `PAgentSchema.sharedStoreURL()`.
  -->
  <key>com.apple.security.application-groups</key>
  <array>
    <string>group.com.example.PAgent</string>
  </array>

  <!--
    Deliberately absent:

    - `com.apple.developer.healthkit` — Phase 3 (§10). R4 ranks it last
      because it carries the most review friction, and nothing before it
      depends on it.
    - `com.apple.developer.icloud-*` — §9.3 forbids writing health data to
      iCloud, and `SignalEvent` holds every source in one table, so the store
      is built with `cloudKitDatabase: .none`. Having no iCloud entitlement at
      all means the compliance guarantee cannot be undone by a container
      setting changed later.
  -->
</dict>
</plist>
